   "body": "## Goal\nThe protocol design is complete and reviewed: re-review #166 recommended accept for the F1/F2 revision at 2669ec4 (benchmark/protocol.md; tasks section lines 7-44). The `tasks` and `oracle` milestones are still `not-started`. Expected gain: the first substantive development evidence for `tasks` (and development keys for `oracle`) on public cases, following the protocol's case specification, gradeable answer record, frozen reference inventory and denominator rules. The next Policymaker checks: the case files and generator exist, `--check` reproduces them, preparation-status.json marks `tasks` as prepared-not-reviewed, and the report gives case counts per family plus the items whose native reference is unavailable; it then orders a cross-family review.\n\n## Acceptance\n- [ ] Write `benchmark/scripts/dev_cases.py` (stdlib only, `python3 -I`, no import or execution of prepared source code) that derives reference facts directly from original XML in the prepared read-only sources (`$LABVIEW_ENVIRONMENT`) and writes `benchmark/cases/dev/*.json`; `--check` reproduces existing files byte-identically and never writes. Run both modes and report exit codes.\n- [ ] 8-12 development cases from the families whose structure is readable from original XML without native LabVIEW: `.lvproj` (declared items, dependencies, build specifications; declarations separate from resolution), `.lvclass` (inheritance, membership, scope), `.lvlib` (membership, scope). Add at least one `.vi` case specification whose native oracle is explicitly `unavailable`, so the gap stays visible.\n- [ ] Each case follows the protocol: question, artifact identity (repository, commit, path, archive member chain, sha256 matching benchmark/sources.json or coverage.json), family and save version, required answer-claim IDs with atomic facts, conditions and XML-path locators, required reader-observation IDs, acceptable alternatives, disallowed inferences, uncertainties, and fixed denominators per dimension. Claims that need native evidence (resolution, behavior, diagrams) are marked unverified/native-required, never asserted from XML alone.\n- [ ] Record a lineage/group identifier per case (same project, archive copies, variants) so a later split cannot place copies on both sides. All these cases are public development cases; state that none can be withheld.\n- [ ] Pin the new files under `tasks` (and keys under `oracle` if separate) in benchmark/preparation.json with correct sha256; set those milestones in preparation-status.json to `prepared-not-reviewed`; keep every file under 2 MB.\n- [ ] Do not write product code, quality thresholds, the LabVIEW 2024 fallback, scope reduction or a readiness claim. Report missing coverage (families, versions, native evidence) explicitly.\n## Verification\n`python3 -I policy/evaluator/evaluate.py --check`\n## Context\n- benchmark/protocol.md `## tasks` and `## oracle`: required case/inventory format and scoring rules.\n- docs/perpetua/evaluation.md: milestone requirements (sources, tasks, oracle).\n- benchmark/sources.md, benchmark/coverage.md: prepared public sources, artifact families and gaps.\n- execution/roles/benchmark/SKILL.md: pinning rule for preparation.json.\n## Budget\nNever read benchmark/sources.json or coverage.json whole (0.4 MB / 1.8 MB): query them with bounded `python3 -I` summaries. Read only the protocol sections named above, not resources-proposal.md, engine code or research. Write .session/report.md from the start; if the budget runs short, save the generator and finished cases and report `partial` with a Successor.",
